x}}\\mathbf{a}\\left(\\mathit{x}\\right)\\mathbf{a}{\\left(\\mathit{x}\\right)}^{H}+{N}_{0}\\mathbf{I}$, and the total information is the sum over bins, where $\\mathbf{a}\\left(\\mathit{x}\\right)$ is the (frequency-dependent) manifold vector whose parameter dependence is exactly that of the deterministic model. ### 6.2. Practical Regime of the Unknown-Waveform Case\n\nThe factor $\\gamma /(1+\\gamma )$ is innocuous only when the per-snapshot array SNR $\\gamma $ is not small, and, for a wideband long capture, it is very small: with $N=BT$ samples per station and pass SNR $\\rho $, the per-sample SNR is ${\\rho }_{s}=\\rho /N$ and $\\gamma =M{\\rho }_{s}$. For the scenario of Section 7 ($B=2$ MHz, $T=60$ s, $N\\approx 1.2\\times {10}^{8}$, $M=3$) a pass SNR of 15 dB corresponds to ${\\rho }_{s}\\approx -66$ dB and $\\gamma \\approx 8\\times {10}^{-7}$, so the unknown-waveform bound is about ${10}^{3}$ times the deterministic one, and cross-station correlation of a Gaussian waveform is above its detection threshold only when $N{\\rho }_{s}^{2}\\gg 1$, i.e., for pass SNRs of the order of 45–50 dB. The unknown-waveform direct estimator is therefore viable for strong narrowband or short captures (high ${\\rho }_{s}$), whereas, at low-pass SNRs where the single-step advantage of Section 7 is predicted, the waveform must be known or at least structured (a known modulation format, a pilot, or a cyclostationary signature), which is the usual situation for a non-cooperative but standard-compliant downlink. The low-SNR results of this paper are accordingly stated for the known-waveform model, and the extension of the efficiency demonstration to structured-waveform estimators is left to future work.\n\nThe numerical non-cooperative signal model retains delay/Doppler geometry and does not exploit carrier-phase ranging. Although the signal model (3) contains the carrier-phase term ${e}^{-j2\\pi {f}_{c}{\\tau }_{m}\\left(t\\right)}$, the unknown constant complex gain ${b}_{m}$ absorbs the absolute per-station carrier phase; the estimator therefore exploits the time-varying phase evolution (Doppler and Doppler rate), not absolute carrier-phase ranging. This is what the “delay/Doppler” description of the numerical observable model (Section 7) refers to.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPractical Limitations of the Known Waveform DoA Estimation Algorithm | Biblioteca da SBrT (https://biblioteca.sbrt.org.br/articles/5180)\nciteturn230search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Known-waveform (KW) direction-of-arrival estimators exploit a known reference signal to improve spatial selectivity, but their practical performance depends on operating conditions that have not been systematically quantified. ... We adopt a packet-based observation model in which the known preamble occupies a finite portion of the received buffer so that synchronization errors shift the correlation window into pre-arrival noise or unknown payload, and we evaluate performance via Monte Carlo trials on a compact uniform circular array.Results show that the KW approach retains a clear advantage over classical blind methods under unstructured interference; it remains largely robust across common structured interferer modulations but exhibits measurable residual sensitivity dependent on symbol alphabet and pulse shaping; its performance degrades sharply outside a narrow synchronization tolerance around correct alignment; and a regime-dependent minimum training length is required before KW gains appear.\n\n# Practical Limitations of the Known Waveform DoA Estimation Algorithm\n\nNicolas Souza de Melo de Oliveira, José Antonio Apolinário Jr., Claudio Augusto Saunders Filho\n\nDirection-of-arrival estimation known waveform interference rejection synchronization error\n\n## Resumo\n\nKnown-waveform (KW) direction-of-arrival estimators exploit a known reference signal to improve spatial selectivity, but their practical performance depends on operating conditions that have not been systematically quantified. This paper provides a rigorous simulation-backed characterization of three such conditions: interferer modulation format, synchronization error between the received signal and the local reference, and available training-sequence length. We adopt a packet-based observation model in which the known preamble occupies a finite portion of the received buffer so that synchronization errors shift the correlation window into pre-arrival noise or unknown payload, and we evaluate performance via Monte Carlo trials on a compact uniform circular array. Results show that the KW approach retains a clear advantage over classical blind methods under unstructured interference; it remains largely robust across common structured interferer modulations but exhibits measurable residual sensitivity dependent on symbol alphabet and pulse shaping; its performance degrades sharply outside a narrow synchronization tolerance around correct alignment; and a regime-dependent minimum training length is required before KW gains appear. Together, these results delineate the operating envelope for KW spatial processing and yield concrete guidelines: prefer KW estimators when synchronization can be maintained within the identified tolerance and sufficient preamble length is available; otherwise classical covariance-based approaches may be preferable.\n\nBaixar PDF\n\nDOI\n    10.14209/sbrt.2026.1571299571\nEvento\n    XLIV Simpósio Brasileiro de Telecomunicações e Processamento de Sinais (SBrT2026)\nAno--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirect position determination algorithm for non‐circular sources in the presence of mutual coupling and its theoretical performance analysis - Deng - 2023 - IET Signal Processing - Wiley Online Library (https://ietresearch.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1049/sil2.12193)\nciteturn230search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; In addition, the theoretical mean square error expression for the position estimations of the proposed algorithm under the influence of finite sampling is derived based on the matrix perturbation analysis theory, and the corresponding Cramér-Rao bound is given. ... In contrast to the traditional two-step localisation model [1, 2], scholars represented by Weiss and Amar [3-5] have proposed a novel direct position determination (DPD) method, the basic idea of which is to estimate the position information of the target directly from the original signal sampling through corresponding processing, without estimating other intermediate parameters. ... To broaden the application of our algorithm, we utilise both the symmetric Toeplitz property of the uniform linear matrix and the cyclic Toeplitz property of the uniform circular matrix to derive a unified extended rank reduction estimation model in combination with the non-circular properties of the signals and achieve a “decoupled” estimation of the parameters of both the target position and the array inter-coupling errors.\n## Abstract\n\nThis article proposes a direct position determination (DPD) algorithm for non-circular sources observed by a moving array using the self-calibration technique in the presence of mutual coupling. The method first utilises the symmetric Toeplitz property of uniform linear array matrices with mutual coupling and cyclic Toeplitz property of uniform circular array coupling matrix, realising the decoupled estimations of target position parameters and sensor error parameters. Then the position parameters of multiple non-circular are directly determined based on the subspace data fusion criterion in a decoupled manner, where the subspaces are obtained using the extended array data model with the non-circular properties of the sources. This results in a significant improvement in the accuracy of the target position estimation and the number of distinguishable sources compared to the traditional mutual coupling calibration algorithm. In addition, the theoretical mean square error expression for the position estimations of the proposed algorithm under the influence of finite sampling is derived based on the matrix perturbation analysis theory, and the corresponding Cramér-Rao bound is given. Finally, the correctness of the theoretical derivation and the superiority of the method is verified by simulation experiments.\n\n## 1 INTRODUCTION\n\nThe localisation of radiation or scattering sources occupies an essential place in the field of electronic countermeasures, and a more mature theory has been developed. In contrast to the traditional two-step localisation model [1, 2], scholars represented by Weiss and Amar [3-5] have proposed a novel direct position determination (DPD) method, the basic idea of which is to estimate the position information of the target directly from the original signal sampling through corresponding processing, without estimating other intermediate parameters. Research in recent years has shown that the DPD approaches have higher localisation accuracy and lower resolution thresholds at low signal-to-noise ratios (SNRs) and small number of samples [6, 7].\n\nThe DPD methods can further improve the target localisation accuracy by combining the waveform characteristics information of the signal [8]. [13]. Despite its progress, to the best of our knowledge, it is noteworthy that most of the existing DPDs for non-circular signals assume that the observer arrays work ideally, whereas in practice the mutual coupling effect between the elements can degrade the positioning accuracy or even render the algorithms ineffective. As a result, it is necessary to investigate mutual coupling error calibration techniques to improve the performance of DPD methods further [14, 15].\n\nThe rank reduction estimator (RARE) [16] is one kind of the widely used sensor error calibration algorithms. It utilises the unique structure of the array flow vector to decompose it into the product of two-part vectors, where one is related only to the target position, and the other contains only the error parameters. This can realise self-calibration with a joint online estimation of the target position and the sensor error parameters in a decoupled manner. Due to its advantages of requiring no information on specific error parameters, high estimation accuracy, and no need for high-dimensional optimisation search, different RARE self-calibration algorithms have been designed to calibrate errors based on different kinds of arrays. Ye et al. [17] decomposed uniform rectangular array into several parallel uniform linear arrays, and developed a two-dimensional DOA estimation algorithm. For the mutual coupling effect in uniform hexagon array, Liu et al. [18] proposed two mutual coupling self-calibration algorithms.\n\nBased on the above research basis, this paper considers a localisation scenario using a moving observer array and proposes a self-calibration DPD algorithm for non-circular sources with maximum non-circularity rate in the presence of mutual coupling error. To broaden the application of our algorithm, we utilise both the symmetric Toeplitz property of the uniform linear matrix and the cyclic Toeplitz property of the uniform circular matrix to derive a unified extended rank reduction estimation model in combination with the non-circular properties of the signals and achieve a “decoupled” estimation of the parameters of both the target position and the array inter-coupling errors. The matrix perturbation principle is used to derive the Cramér-Rao bound (CRB) of target position and sensor error parameters for the independent non-circular sources with maximum non-circularity rate. In addition, the closed expression of the theoretical mean square error (MSE) of our method is presented, and the identifiable conditions of the algorithm parameters are also analysed.\n\nThe rest of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 presents the signal model based on a single observer array in the presence of mutual coupling errors. Section 3 proposes the improved DPD method. Section 4 analyses the performance of the proposed method. Section 5 provides a series of numerical simulations and makes the discussion. Finally, the conclusion is drawn in Section 6.\n\nSome necessary mathematical notation and symbols used throughout the paper are explained in Table 1 before entering the main text.\n\nTABLE 1. Mathematical notation and symbols.\n\nNotation and symbol  | Description\n--- | ---\n, ,  | The conjugate transpose, transpose, conjugate of a matrix or vector\n,  | Diagonal and block diagonal matrices formed by vector elements\n,  | -dimensional sets of complex and real numbers\n | Determinant of a matrix\n | Mathematical expectation notation\n | The row and column elements of the matrix\n,  | The real and imaginary parts of a complex number\n,  | Kronecker matrix product and Column-wise Kronecker matrix product\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmaximum a posteriori probability time-delay estimation for seismic signals | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/198/3/1543/587752)\nciteturn230search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.3 years ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; In order to express the time-delay estimation as an optimization problem, it is necessary to find a cost function whose argument is the time delay. ... C_{τ} = σ_{0}I is the a priori model covariance matrix, where σ_{0} is the a priori time delay standard deviation. ... The Cauchy distribution provides an accurate model of the hand-picked traveltime difference pdf (Fig. 3a) used as a priori information in eq.\nC_{τ} = σ_{0}I is the a priori model covariance matrix, where σ_{0} is the a priori time delay standard deviation. I is the identity matrix.\n\nThe time delay between two signals is given by the abscissa of the minimum of the cos